{"agent":"recover-6a","data":{"by":"grok-4.7","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"`test_noop_retriage_keeps_the_pass_on_refutation` claims the `<=` confirmed-count guard is a no-op that must keep the pass, but the call is `_tri(repo, 2, \"refuted\", ...)`. `_revoke_refutation_pass` returns \"\" as soon as `verdict != \"confirmed\"`, so this input never reaches the count comparison. Deleting the guard, or revoking on every `confirmed` re-triage even when `live[\"confirmed\"]` does not exceed `flag[\"confirmed\"]` (gate already passed on refutation with that finding already counted), still leaves both tests green.","digest":"460905abcd1e160c","family":"xai","location":"tests/test_review_triage_unpasses_on_reconfirm.py:48","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"LOW","title":"tests/test_review_triage_unpasses_on_reconfirm.py:48"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"Removing the `live[\"confirmed\"] <= flag[\"confirmed\"]` skip makes every `verdict == \"confirmed\"` re-triage strip `passed_on_refutation` from every flag in this loop, not only the gate whose confirmed set grew. `triage_counts(it, g)` is per item and gate, and the old `continue` was the only filter after the `dict` check. Given item/gate B already passed on refutation with `flag[\"confirmed\"] == 0`, and a re-triage to confirmed of a finding that `triage_counts` counts only for gate A (so B stays at 0, which is not greater than the snapshot), the new code still builds `ev` without `passed_on_refutation` and records it for B. The same miss applies when the finding was already confirmed and the live count does not exceed the snapshot. The early return on non-confirmed verdicts does not make this comparison dead on the confirmed path; the no-op test still calls `_tri(..., \"refuted\")` and never reaches it.","digest":"a27ac6e23017a3d7","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/api/review/triaging.py:521","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/review/triaging.py:521"}],"chunks_total":1,"coverage":"1/